"""The aerodynamic table a real flight swept off the real airframe.

Transcribed from ``logs/LOG2747``'s ``aero M=...`` lines -- a flight of the
committed configuration that flew the cone, rolled out and **stopped 2220 m
along the runway, 5.9 m off the centreline**.  So this is not a probe's
opinion about the aircraft; it is what the aircraft reported about itself on
a flight that worked, and the landing constants were fitted against flights
exactly like it.

It exists because ``spaceplane/tests/fakeplane``'s own table comes from
``planeprobe``, which over-reads subsonic lift by about 1.8x (spaceplane
failure 13) -- fine for flying the offline sim, useless for asking whether a
derived landing constant lands on the value that was fitted in game.  A
derivation checked against the wrong polar is checked against nothing.

Transcribed, and therefore exactly the thing this project distrusts.  It is
allowed here because it is a **pin, not a flight constant**: nothing flies
it, one test reads it, and if it ever disagrees with the aircraft the answer
is to re-take it from a current log rather than to adjust what it is
compared against.  ``mass`` is that flight's landing mass, off its "at the
landing mass" line, because a glide ratio at the wrong weight is a glide
ratio for a different aircraft.
"""

